#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128882 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b049dea4-5045-57ec-ad6a-c49f30e5da1f" :knowledge-point/title "Converting a Matrix to Rotation-Scaling Form Given Multiple Parts of the Decomposition"}] :question/id #uuid "1b13d9bb-9bcf-50be-a58d-39e82d99b9bf" :question/math-academy-id "e-5530" :question/problem "$\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 + 2\\textrm{i}\\end{bmatrix}$\nConsider the vector $\\mathbf{v}$ and the rotation-scaling matrix $C$ shown above. Given that $\\mathbf{v}$ is an eigenvector of a $2 \\times 2$ matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda=-1-4\\textrm{i}$, find a matrix $P$ such that $A=PCP^{-1}$." :question/worked-solution "Recall that for any $2 \\times 2$ real matrix $A$ with complex eigenvalues, we have $A=PCP^{-1}$, where\n\n$$\n[ \\mid \\mid \\mid; Re(v) \\mid Im(v); \\mid \\mid \\mid ]\n$$\n\nand $\\mathbf{v}$ is an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to the complex eigenvalue $\\lambda$.\n\nFirst, notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}Re(λ) & Im(λ) \\\\ -Im(λ) & Re(λ)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNext, we can find the real and imaginary parts of our eigenvector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nv &= \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 + 2\\textrm{i}\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}_(⏟)_(Re(v)) + \\textrm{i}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}_(⏟)_(Im(v))\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can now construct $P$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP &= [ \\mid \\mid \\mid \\\\\n&= Re(v) \\mid Im(v) \\\\\n&= \\mid \\mid \\mid ] = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128883 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1dcdd3f4-c03e-5887-a493-05598dbe4fc3" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Eigenvalues and Eigenvectors of a Matrix in Rotation-Scaling Form"}] :question/id #uuid "26e641c9-bda5-5b93-8137-f4ec744ddb5c" :question/math-academy-id "e-5531" :question/problem "$\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$\nConsider the matrix $P$ and the rotation-scaling matrix $C$ shown above. Given that $A=PCP^{-1}$, what are the eigenvalues and the corresponding eigenvectors of $A$?" :question/worked-solution "Recall that for any $2 \\times 2$ real matrix $A$ with complex eigenvalues, we have $A=PCP^{-1}$, where\n\n$$\n[ \\mid \\mid \\mid; Re(v) \\mid Im(v); \\mid \\mid \\mid ]\n$$\n\nand $\\mathbf{v}$ is an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to the complex eigenvalue $\\lambda$.\n\n- From the matrix $\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ - (-1) & 3\\end{bmatrix}$ we deduce that $\\textrm{Re}(\\lambda)={\\color{blue}3}$ and $\\textrm{Im}(\\lambda) = {\\color{red}-1}$. So, our eigenvalue is\n$\\lambda = {\\color{blue}3}+({\\color{red}-1})\\text{i} = 3-\\text{i}$.\n- From the matrix $\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$ we deduce that our corresponding eigenvector is\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 + (-1)\\textrm{i} \\\\ 1 + 0\\textrm{i}\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nFinally, the second eigenvalue of $A$ must be the complex conjugate of $\\lambda$, and the second eigenvector must be the complex conjugate of $\\mathbf{v}$.\n\nTherefore, our eigenvalues and eigenvectors are\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 ± \\textrm{i} \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128884 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "148c97a4-24c1-5289-ad80-bee4af34509b" :knowledge-point/title "Converting a Matrix to Rotation-Scaling Form Given Only One Eigenvalue"}] :question/id #uuid "75f39ec3-0963-5555-b418-d5b2df3be0ec" :question/math-academy-id "e-5532" :question/problem "$\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -5 \\\\ 9 & -5\\end{bmatrix}$\nConsider the matrices shown above.\nGiven that $A$ has the complex eigenvalue $\\lambda=-2+6\\textrm{i}$, and that $A=PCP^{-1}$ where $C$ is a rotation-scaling matrix, find the matrix $P$." :question/worked-solution "Recall that for any $2 \\times 2$ real matrix $A$ with complex eigenvalues, we have $A=PCP^{-1}$, where\n\n$$\n[ \\mid \\mid \\mid; Re(v) \\mid Im(v); \\mid \\mid \\mid ]\n$$\n\nand $\\mathbf{v}$ is an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to the complex eigenvalue $\\lambda$.\n\nSince we are given the eigenvalue\n\n$$\n\\lambda=-2+6\\textrm{i}\n$$\n\nwe can immediately construct the rotation-scaling matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 6 \\\\ -6 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTo compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions to the matrix equation\n\n$$\n(A - \\lambda I) \\textbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\,\\lambda_1 =-2+6\\textrm{i}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA - λI &= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -5 \\\\ 9 & -5\\end{bmatrix} - \\begin{bmatrix}-2 + 6\\textrm{i} & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 + 6\\textrm{i}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}3 - 6\\textrm{i} & -5 \\\\ 9 & -3 - 6\\textrm{i}\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the homogeneous system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{(3 - 6\\textrm{i})x_{1} - 5x_{2} = 0; 9x_{1} + (-3 - 6\\textrm{i})x_{2} = 0\n$$\n\nSince the matrix $A-\\lambda I$ is singular, the system above must have a non-zero solution. This means that one equation of the system is a multiple of another. As a result, we can drop one of the rows and take one of the variables as the free variable. For example, from the second equation, we get\n\n$$\nx_1=\\dfrac 1 3(1+2\\textrm{i})x_2\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{3}(1 + 2\\textrm{i})x_{2} \\\\ x_{2}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSetting $x_2=3$, we get the eigenvector\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 + 2\\textrm{i} \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can construct the matrix $P$ as follows:\n\n$$\nP = [ \\mid \\mid \\mid; Re(v) \\mid Im(v); \\mid \\mid \\mid ] = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128968 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "88f3ba21-6407-503e-aec0-fa4462a69e73" :knowledge-point/title "Converting a Matrix to Rotation-Scaling Form Using the Complex Conjugate"}] :question/id #uuid "e0469305-b1d9-5430-9911-2f0c209b3264" :question/math-academy-id "e-8734" :question/problem "$\\begin{bmatrix}-2\\textrm{i} \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}$\nConsider the vector $\\mathbf{v}$ and the rotation-scaling matrix $C$ shown above. Given that $\\mathbf{v}$ is an eigenvector of a $2 \\times 2$ matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda=2+4\\textrm{i}$, find a matrix $P$ such that $A=PCP^{-1}$." :question/worked-solution "Recall that for any $2 \\times 2$ real matrix $A$ with complex eigenvalues, we have $A=PCP^{-1}$, where\n\n$$\n[ \\mid \\mid \\mid; Re(v) \\mid Im(v); \\mid \\mid \\mid ]\n$$\n\nand $\\mathbf{v}$ is an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to the complex eigenvalue $\\lambda$.\n\nFirst, notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}Re(λ) & Im(λ) \\\\ -Im(λ) & Re(λ)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nwhile\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}Re(λ^(―)) & Im(λ^(―)) \\\\ -Im(λ^(―)) & Re(λ^(―))\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSo, our rotation-scaling matrix $C$ corresponds to the eigenvalue\n\n$$\n\\overline{\\lambda}=2-4\\textrm{i}\n$$\n\nthe complex conjugate of $\\lambda$. Hence, to construct the matrix $P$, we use the eigenvector $\\overline{\\mathbf{v}}$, the complex conjugate of $\\mathbf{v}$.\n\nNext, we find the real and imaginary parts of our eigenvector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nv^(―) &= \\begin{bmatrix}2\\textrm{i} \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}_(⏟)_(Re(v^(―))) + \\textrm{i}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}_(⏟)_(Im(v^(―)))\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can now construct $P$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP &= [ \\mid \\mid \\mid \\\\\n&= Re(v^(―)) \\mid Im(v^(―)) \\\\\n&= \\mid \\mid \\mid ] = \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ 7 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129601 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b049dea4-5045-57ec-ad6a-c49f30e5da1f" :knowledge-point/title "Converting a Matrix to Rotation-Scaling Form Given Multiple Parts of the Decomposition"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "86bb7c33-7d70-54c1-b928-ed8e10c801fe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127401} {:answer/id #uuid "ab544de7-81aa-5555-8083-2ab360835387" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127402} {:answer/id #uuid "bb6b8f11-ee8e-59c2-b126-7a746a53bb84" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127403} {:answer/id #uuid "4c091f46-17b3-5fd9-bed7-4e4049c71a92" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127404} {:answer/id #uuid "786de4d1-a3f6-5c49-8dc0-053bc311ab8c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127405}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "86bb7c33-7d70-54c1-b928-ed8e10c801fe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127401} :answer-field/id #uuid "de1c1000-619a-54f4-bcb4-207769cbe877" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127400}] :question/id #uuid "31a3b63f-4053-528b-a2b8-f0ec571660f1" :question/math-academy-id "q-38557" :question/problem "$v = \\begin{bmatrix}-\\textrm{i} \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}, C = \\begin{bmatrix}3 & 4 \\\\ -4 & 3\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nConsider the vector $v$ and the rotation-scaling matrix $C$ shown above. Given that $v$ is an eigenvector of a $2 \\cdot 2$ matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $λ = 3 + 4\\textrm{i}$, find a matrix $P$ such that $A = PCP^{-1}$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129603 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "148c97a4-24c1-5289-ad80-bee4af34509b" :knowledge-point/title "Converting a Matrix to Rotation-Scaling Form Given Only One Eigenvalue"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a2296836-18b7-53d1-8414-1285ed5dd45a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127413} {:answer/id #uuid "bc3f903b-cd0a-50de-98dd-aa8f1dec7f4f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127414} {:answer/id #uuid "ee52169d-ba3c-5566-a06d-23ad89855e99" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127415} {:answer/id #uuid "05b74c73-71da-5f93-95d1-0185e948ad1f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127416} {:answer/id #uuid "35b27bd1-f7ed-5008-9fdb-871022568711" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127417}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ee52169d-ba3c-5566-a06d-23ad89855e99" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127415} :answer-field/id #uuid "0cbec3dd-751d-527b-b37c-9f17e84ba1df" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127412}] :question/id #uuid "7d6641ce-39fa-5304-84ca-9071211edd5b" :question/math-academy-id "q-38565" :question/problem "$A = \\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 2 \\\\ -5 & -1\\end{bmatrix}, P = \\begin{bmatrix}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above.\nGiven that $A = PCP^{-1}$, where $C$ is a rotation-scaling matrix, what is the value of\n$\\frac{a^{2} + b^{2}}{c^{2} + d^{2}}$?"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129651 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1dcdd3f4-c03e-5887-a493-05598dbe4fc3" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Eigenvalues and Eigenvectors of a Matrix in Rotation-Scaling Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a44df2bc-f14f-59a2-a672-6f0e6bb9920f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$λ = 5∓2\\textrm{i}$ and $v = \\begin{bmatrix}-1 ± 4\\textrm{i} \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127701} {:answer/id #uuid "df507133-b25f-5b28-ba47-8c4a2f3d63c0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$λ = 2 ± 5\\textrm{i}$ and $v = \\begin{bmatrix}-1 ± 4\\textrm{i} \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127702} {:answer/id #uuid "12c35eb9-f22b-5303-ba37-a36c95e38a27" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$λ =-1∓4\\textrm{i}$ and $v = \\begin{bmatrix}2 ± 5\\textrm{i} \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127703} {:answer/id #uuid "137bdf37-6094-5b37-bef7-d0150c3ee24f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$λ = 5∓2\\textrm{i}$ and $v = \\begin{bmatrix}4 ± \\textrm{i} \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127704} {:answer/id #uuid "1ea63856-d4a2-5c1e-848c-4ccd15dd67b0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$λ =-1 ± 4\\textrm{i}$ and $v = \\begin{bmatrix}5 ± 2\\textrm{i} \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127705}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a44df2bc-f14f-59a2-a672-6f0e6bb9920f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$λ = 5∓2\\textrm{i}$ and $v = \\begin{bmatrix}-1 ± 4\\textrm{i} \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127701} :answer-field/id #uuid "691b3425-43de-571e-82e4-0da7a888fe7c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127700}] :question/id #uuid "a0d796fd-8159-54fb-b817-7bb654ebaf53" :question/math-academy-id "q-39130" :question/problem "$P = \\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}, C = \\begin{bmatrix}5 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nConsider the matrix $P$ and the rotation-scaling matrix $C$ shown above. Given that $A = PCP^{-1}$, what are the eigenvalues and the corresponding eigenvectors of $A$?"}]}
